\begin{table*}[hbt]
\ifnextchar[{\eatarg}{}


{\caption{{Three distinct sources of scale-related problems for
interaction claims.}{\label{tbl-family-link-metric}}}
\vspace{-20pt}}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 6\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2500}}@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
Problem
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
Key question
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
Psychological example
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
Risk for interaction claims
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\endlastfoot
Wrong family & Does the model represent the support or sampling process
of the outcome? & Counts, proportions, or trial accuracies modeled as
unbounded Gaussian observations. & The fitted model may impose
impossible predictions or miss the mean-variance relation, so curvature
or constraints can appear as moderation. \\
Correct family, wrong link & Given the outcome family, which scale
defines additivity? & Forced-choice accuracy modeled with a standard
binomial logit, even though the task has a known chance floor such as
.50. & The product term tests additivity on the wrong scale, so a
pseudo-interaction can arise even when the correct link-scale model has
only main effects. \\
Wrong measurement metric & Does the recorded score have an interpretable
interval scale for the claim being made? & Likert items or sum scores
treated as raw interval outcomes without justification. & A product term
may reflect thresholds, discreteness, bounds, or scale compression
rather than moderation of the underlying construct. \\
\end{longtable}

\end{table*}
\efloatseparator
 
\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3333}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3333}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\linewidth - 4\tabcolsep) * \real{0.3333}}@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
\begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
Alternative
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
Why it helps
\end{minipage} & \begin{minipage}[b]{\linewidth}\raggedright
When it fails
\end{minipage} \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\endlastfoot
Cumulative-link or ordered-probit/logit model & It treats the outcome as
ordered categories and models threshold crossing rather than equal
raw-score distances (\citeproc{ref-agresti2010}{Agresti, 2010};
\citeproc{ref-burknervuorre2019}{Bürkner \& Vuorre, 2019};
\citeproc{ref-smith2020}{Smith et al., 2020}). & It can be awkward for
long totals, may rely on proportional-odds or related assumptions, and
may still be too coarse if the claim concerns item-level functioning. \\
Item-level ordinal or IRT-inspired model & It separates the latent
construct from item thresholds, item difficulties, and item
discrimination, so floor, ceiling, and uneven item sensitivity can be
represented directly. & It needs item-level data, stronger measurement
assumptions, larger samples, and attention to local dependence,
differential item functioning, and model fit. \\
Binomial, beta-binomial, or Poisson-binomial approximation & It can be
useful when the score is a count of successes, symptoms, or
endorsements, and it respects discreteness and bounds. Beta-binomial
versions can absorb overdispersion, and Poisson-binomial versions can
allow heterogeneous item probabilities. & A simple binomial model may
wrongly treat items as exchangeable trials. These models may also be
poor for ordinal ratings or for items that do not define comparable
successes. \\
Robust and sensitivity analyses & They show whether the interaction
conclusion depends on the manifest sum-score metric, for example by
comparing raw-score, ordinal, bounded-count, and item-level analyses. &
They do not identify the true scale by themselves. If plausible analyses
disagree, the substantive conclusion must be stated as
scale-dependent. \\
\end{longtable}
